# The UTAG procfs. utags.ko creates /proc/hw (per-SKU hardware descriptor) and
# /proc/config (per-unit factory config); both were falling through to the
# generic u:object_r:proc:s0.
#
# Taken verbatim from stock's vendor_sepolicy.cil:
#   (genfscon proc "/config" (u object_r proc_hwconf ((s0) (s0))))
#   (genfscon proc "/hw"     (u object_r proc_hwconf ((s0) (s0))))
#
# genfscon matches by longest path prefix, so "/config" here does NOT capture
# /proc/config.gz -- AOSP's own `genfscon proc /config.gz u:object_r:config_gz:s0`
# is longer and still wins.
genfscon proc /hw     u:object_r:proc_hwconf:s0
genfscon proc /config u:object_r:proc_hwconf:s0

# Motorola's power-supply nodes. Four of the eight entries under
# /sys/class/power_supply were falling through to generic u:object_r:sysfs:s0
# (checked with `ls -ldZ` on the resolved /sys/devices paths); the other four
# already come from QCOM's sepolicy_vndr. The consequence was a live denial --
# AOSP's health HAL reads each supply's `type` file while enumerating
# /sys/class/power_supply, and was denied getattr/open/read on two of them:
#   denied { read } for comm="android.hardwar" name="type"
#     scontext=u:r:hal_health_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# The first two are taken verbatim from stock's vendor_sepolicy.cil (lines 176
# and 36). The last two have no stock entry -- stock's kernel exposes the i2c
# bus at /devices/platform/soc/a84000.i2c/... while ours nests it under the
# qupv3 wrapper, so stock's paths do not match here -- but they are real
# supplies on this unit (the charger IC and the razr's second cell), so they
# get the same types their function implies.
#
# batt_health already holds rw on vendor_sysfs_battery_supply (batt_health.te),
# so relabelling mmi_battery does not disturb its update_now write. This
# supersedes the note in batt_health.te about avoiding stock's genfscon: the
# reason given there was that healthd walks these nodes, and the correct answer
# is to label them per stock AND grant the health HAL the read it needs
# (hal_health_default.te), not to leave them generic.
# EXACTLY stock's Motorola supplies. Do not add the two i2c ones -- it is not an
# oversight in stock, it is IMPOSSIBLE, and three flashed cold boots proved it.
#
# THE i2c SYSFS PATH IS NOT STABLE ACROSS BOOTS. The charger and flip_battery
# supplies live behind the qupv3 i2c controller, and the bus renumbers:
#
#   boot A: .../a84000.i2c/i2c-1/1-0052/power_supply/charger
#   boot B: .../a84000.i2c/i2c-2/2-0052/power_supply/charger
#
# and their wakeup children renumber too (charger/wakeup21 -> wakeup31,
# flip_battery/wakeup8 -> wakeup29). genfscon matches a FIXED path prefix, so no
# entry can track them. Labelling them appears to work exactly when the bus
# happens to enumerate the way it did when the path was copied down.
#
# That is what made the measurements look contradictory. Denials per cold boot:
#
#   labels             | hal_health_default | system_suspend | note
#   -------------------+--------------------+----------------+---------------------
#   4 supplies         |          0         |      110       | boot enumerated i2c-1
#   stock's 2          |        162         |      110       | -
#   4 + wakeup entries |        164         |      110       | boot enumerated i2c-2
#
# The "0" was luck, not a fix. system_suspend never moved at all -- its 110 are
# the wakeup children of those same unstable paths, equally unlabelable, and
# stock carries them too.
#
# ACCEPTED RESIDUAL: hal_health_default takes ~160 benign denials per boot reading
# `type` on the two i2c supplies while enumerating /sys/class/power_supply. It is
# log noise only -- battery reporting is unaffected and comes from the correctly
# labelled `battery` node (verified on device: level/voltage/temp/technology all
# populate). Deliberately NOT silenced with dontaudit: that would also hide real
# future denials for this domain on plain sysfs.
#
# QCOM's sepolicy_vndr already ships sysfs_wakeup for battery/usb/wireless/ucsi1;
# adding those here is a hard build error ("duplicate entry for genfs entry").
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:mmi,charger/power_supply/mmi_battery u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_battery_supply:s0
# NOTE: the wakeupNN suffix was dropped from this entry when the wakeup-source
# survey below was done. It was copied from stock as ".../mmi_battery/wakeup30",
# and NN is a global registration counter that changes between boots -- the same
# instability this file already documents for the i2c paths. The parent `wakeup`
# directory is stable and prefix-matching covers every child, which is what
# stock does for its other ~30 wakeup entries.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:mmi,charger/power_supply/mmi_battery/wakeup u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:qcom,pmic_glink/soc:qcom,pmic_glink:mmi,qti-glink-charger/power_supply/main_battery u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_battery_supply:s0

# /proc/bootinfo. Verbatim from stock (vendor_sepolicy.cil:10). See file.te for
# why this reverses the earlier decision to leave it on the generic proc label,
# and for the list of domains re-granted below.
genfscon proc /bootinfo u:object_r:proc_bootinfo:s0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WAKEUP SOURCES -- the 110 system_suspend denials per boot.
#
# Enumerated on the live device with
#   find /sys/devices -maxdepth 10 -type d -name "wakeup[0-9]*"
# plus `ls -Zd` on each. Eleven sources are walked by
# SystemSuspend::getWakeupStats(); two are already sysfs_wakeup
# (/devices/virtual/fastrpc/adsprpc-smd{,-secure}) and are not denied.
#
# THE PREFIX IS THE PARENT `wakeup` DIRECTORY, NOT THE wakeupNN CHILD. The NN is
# a global registration counter and changes between boots (the note in this file
# about the i2c bus records charger/wakeup21 -> wakeup31 and
# flip_battery/wakeup8 -> wakeup29 across two boots). genfscon does a plain
# longest-prefix match on the path, so naming the parent covers every child
# whatever it is numbered. This is exactly stock's pattern -- around thirty of
# its genfscon entries end in ".../wakeup" -- and two of the six below are
# verbatim stock lines (vendor_sepolicy.cil:80 and :355/:356).
#
# NOTE the qupv3 instance numbers differ from stock: our kernel exposes the SPI
# controllers under ac0000.qcom,qupv3_0_geni_se and 8c0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se
# where stock's line 84 says ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se. The paths below are
# this device's, read off the live filesystem, not stock's.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:qcom,pmic_glink/soc:qcom,pmic_glink:mmi,qti-glink-charger/wakeup u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:fpc_fpc1020/wakeup u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:mmi,charger/wakeup u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/8c0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/88c000.spi/spi_master/spi1/spi1.0/wakeup u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_0_geni_se/a90000.spi/spi_master/spi0/spi0.0/wakeup u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0
#
# THE OTHER THREE CANNOT BE LABELLED, for the reason already established at the
# top of this file -- each sits behind an i2c adapter whose bus number is
# assigned at probe and changes between boots:
#
#   .../884000.i2c/i2c-2/2-0008/wakeup                        (the NFC controller)
#   .../a84000.i2c/i2c-1/1-0052/power_supply/charger/wakeup
#   .../a84000.i2c/i2c-1/1-0064/power_supply/flip_battery/wakeup
#
# A prefix on the controller node itself (884000.i2c / a84000.i2c, which ARE
# stable devicetree names) would survive the renumbering, but it relabels every
# attribute of every client on that bus -- the whole NFC device in one case, and
# in the other the charger/fuel-gauge nodes that hal_health_default and
# vendor_thermal-engine read. That blast radius cannot be checked without a
# flash, and coredomain.te:142 forbids the alternative of just granting
# system_suspend the read.
#
# ACCEPTED RESIDUAL: 30 denials per boot (3 sources x 10 attributes). They are
# wakeup-source STATISTICS; suspend itself does not depend on them, and the
# SPI/fpc/charger sources that DO carry the interesting wakeups are covered
# above. The two labelled-but-denied sources are handled by the read grant in
# system_suspend.te.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# wls_input_current_limit -- the wireless-charging input current cap that
# thermal-engine's te-monitor thread writes as a mitigation step. It was falling
# through to generic sysfs and the write was refused (7 times in the post-flash
# capture):
#
#   avc: denied { write } for comm="te-monitor" name="wls_input_current_limit"
#        dev="sysfs" scontext=u:r:vendor_thermal-engine:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0 tclass=file
#
# VERBATIM STOCK (vendor_sepolicy.cil:41), and the path is a pure devicetree
# path -- no i2c adapter number, no wakeupNN counter -- so unlike the nodes
# discussed above it is stable across boots. Confirmed present at exactly this
# path on the live device.
#
# The two domains that touch it both already hold the type: vendor_thermal-engine
# (write, granted in vendor_thermal-engine.te to match stock) and init_wls, which
# chowns it to system:system -- see the setattr note in init_wls.te, because
# rw_file_perms does NOT include setattr and init_wls got it from the generic
# sysfs rule before this relabel.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:qcom,pmic_glink/soc:qcom,pmic_glink:qcom,battery_charger/wls_input_current_limit u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_battery_supply:s0
